b['law'] = 'Effective January 15, 2003, the Coal Act’s initial-assignment authority continues after the missed October 1, 1993 deadline, although timely performance was mandatory. Lateness alone cannot permanently shift otherwise assignable beneficiaries to unassigned funding. Statutory eligibility, lawful calculation, meaningful notice and review, and any independently supported delay remedy retain their own requirements. Sigmon’s substantive classes are unchanged; Ventura governs unperformed agency factual tasks; Bean’s separate firearms-review prerequisite is neither displaced nor transferred to Coal Act litigation.'
b['separates'] = '**Scalia, joined by O’Connor and Thomas, dissenting.** The dissent would affirm because §9706(a) grants initial-assignment authority in the same time-bounded mandate that requires its exercise. The fixed as-of allocation and express correction and reassignment provisions reinforce closure on October 1, 1993. An initial assignment of a previously unassigned beneficiary differs from a correction authorized under another provision, and the unassigned-beneficiary funding system supplies the statutory destination for those costs. Brock permits statutory context to establish expiration; Good involved separate continuing forfeiture authority and a distinct five-year limitations period. The dissent therefore does not rest on a universal rule that every mandatory agency deadline disables late action. It rejects the Court’s reading of this scheme and would preserve the timing-based judgments. Revenue or fairness cannot enlarge a source of authority Congress bounded by time.'
b['procedure'] = 'Both consolidated timing-based judgments are reversed. Proceedings resume on properly maintained objections to assignments and dependent premiums. The Commissioner must substantiate contested statutory predicates; the reviewing court decides preserved legal objections independently, returning an unperformed statutory factual task to the Commissioner unless the outcome is legally compelled. A late date alone cannot require permanent unassignment.'
