e['holdings'] = '\n\n'.join([
    holding('I. A finite extension of existing copyrights may rationally serve the copyright system',
        'The Copyright Clause permits a finite extension of subsisting copyrights when the extension bears a rational relationship to the promotion of progress through the copyright system; limited times requires bounded duration, not an irrevocable initial expiration date. The twenty-year extension challenged here satisfies that standard through reasonably supported systemwide coordination, dissemination, and preservation purposes, without requiring proof that the additional years newly induced creation of each already completed work.',
        'Part I of the opinion of the Court, joined by O’Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, and Ginsburg. These six adopt the complete standard and its application; the three dissenting grounds are not aggregated into a competing controlling rationale.',
        'The Court holds that finite duration is necessary, while the promotion-of-progress requirement remains a real constitutional limit. A copyright system operates across works, time, and national markets. Congress may reasonably adjust the terms of existing rights as part of international coordination and future dissemination or preservation without claiming to induce completed creation retrospectively. Historical extensions support the conclusion that an initially stated endpoint is not immutable; repetition alone does not establish constitutional power. Feist’s originality boundary concerns what may receive protection and does not fix an unalterable duration. The petitioners correctly identify delayed public access and reject an incentive to create what already exists. Those points do not eliminate the distinct, forward-looking system purposes of a finite adjustment. The present extension rationally serves those purposes; constitutional review does not require the optimal policy or a new work-for-work exchange. Private reward alone would not establish progress, and the decision does not authorize indefinite evasion of limited times. The existing-term application is sustained. The unchallenged new-work term and restoration of public-domain material are outside this case.'),
    holding('II-A. Copyright safeguards do not create categorical First Amendment immunity',
        'Fair use and the distinction between ideas and expression do not categorically immunize copyright-duration legislation from the First Amendment. A challenger must identify the actual expressive burden of the attacked provision, and a court must assess the continuing safeguards in relation to that burden; the petitioners’ delayed reproduction and dissemination of protected expression identify a real burden requiring that inquiry.',
        'Part II-A of the opinion of the Court, joined by O’Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, Ginsburg, and Stone-Zsela. These seven directly support this severable proposition; Stone-Zsela’s join does not reach Part II-B’s constitutional conclusion.',
        'Harper & Row explains the legal work of copyright’s accommodation with expression. Campbell preserves actual fair use through its integrated statutory inquiry, not an owner’s veto over qualifying criticism. Neither makes the copyright label conclusive. Corley separately assessed a demonstrated access-circumvention function and a tailored posting-and-purposeful-linking injunction; it did not remove constitutional review or approve perpetual control of public-domain material. The present burden concerns twenty additional years of exclusivity over expression petitioners wish to reproduce, preserve, or disseminate. Availability of facts, ideas, and some fair uses does not equal the unrestricted uses allowed after expiration. The Court therefore examines that burden rather than treating safeguards as a reason to dispense with inquiry. The different conclusions about the present extension appear in Part II-B and the separate opinions.'),
    holding('II-B. The actual duration change does not violate the First Amendment',
        'The challenged finite extension does not violate the First Amendment because it leaves facts and ideas unprotected, preserves qualifying fair use of expression, and neither compels expression nor imposes a separately content-selected restriction. In this ordinary duration setting those operative safeguards suffice after examination of the actual burden, and the First Amendment does not require additional heightened review of the legislative term policy.',
        'Part II-B of the opinion of the Court, joined by O’Connor, Scalia, Kennedy, Souter, Thomas, and Ginsburg. Stone-Zsela, Stevens, and Breyer withhold assent; their Copyright Clause grounds do not supply affirmative votes for a separate speech-invalidity rule.',
        'The Court recognizes that petitioners’ desired uses are expressive and that fair use will not authorize every reproduction they could make in the public domain. Section 102(b) nevertheless leaves facts and ideas available, and §107 retains a genuine defense for qualifying use of protected expression. Campbell’s four factors remain an integrated inquiry, with the defense burden unchanged and criticism injury distinguished from cognizable market substitution. The term adjustment removes neither safeguard and demands no endorsement or carriage of another’s message. Those features answer the presented burden without a further heightened review of the length Congress selected. Corley’s content-neutral scrutiny addressed the executable circumvention function and its separate injunction; that different restraint does not mechanically prescribe a universal duration test. Quality King concerns exhaustion of distribution rights in particular domestic copies, not reproduction, and Tasini creates no blanket archival privilege. These limits matter to what remains available, but neither reduces the present extension to a categorical immunity theory. The Court rejects the actual speech challenge on this examined statutory setting.')
])
e['precedent'] = '''- Feist Publications, Inc. v. Rural Telephone Service Co., 499 U.S. 340: its constitutional originality boundary is preserved and distinguished from duration; it does not fix the initial expiration date.
- Harper & Row, Publishers, Inc. v. Nation Enterprises, 471 U.S. 539: applied for the relationship between copyright, expression, and fair use; it supplies no categorical immunity from review of a duration burden.
- Campbell v. Acuff-Rose Music, Inc., March 7, 1994: its integrated four-factor analysis, defense burden, and distinction between criticism injury and cognizable substitution remain intact. It supplies an operative safeguard, not unrestricted public-domain use.
- Universal City Studios, Inc. v. Corley, April 23, 2002: distinguished as review of a demonstrated access-circumvention function and a tailored injunction. Its requirement to examine the particular speech burden is preserved; its function-specific scrutiny is not transposed into a universal duration test.
- Quality King Distributors, Inc. v. L’anza Research International, Inc., March 9, 1998: its domestic-copy distribution limit is preserved without creating a reproduction entitlement or deciding term-setting power.
- New York Times Co. v. Tasini, June 25, 2001: its right-specific collective-work rule remains; archival value alone creates neither a statutory reproduction privilege nor a duration rule.'''
e['law'] = 'Effective January 15, 2003, Congress may make a finite adjustment to existing copyright terms when the adjustment rationally serves copyright’s progress function at the system level; the initial expiration date is not constitutionally immutable. The challenged twenty years remain enforceable. The Court also rejects categorical speech immunity, while holding that this duration change’s actual burden is constitutionally accommodated by the retained safeguards without additional heightened term-policy review. Originality, finite duration, the availability of facts and ideas, and genuine fair use remain operative limits. There is no controlling retrospective-immutability rule, no general First Amendment scrutiny tier for all copyright provisions, and no adjudication of the unchallenged new-work term or restoration of public-domain material.'
e['separates'] = '''**Stone-Zsela, concurring in part and dissenting in part.** He joins Part II-A’s rejection of categorical First Amendment immunity but would invalidate the challenged existing-term increment under the Copyright Clause. An extension must genuinely and rationally serve progress; challengers must show that asserted purposes do not connect the additional exclusion to that function. He does not demand empirical proof of a new work-for-work bargain, optimal policy, or immutable original endpoints. Here completed creation cannot be induced; longer lives do not justify added postdeath protection for those works; and generalized harmonization and preservation claims do not explain the undifferentiated increment, which imposes no new dissemination or preservation obligation. Feist supplies a real constitutional boundary; Sony and Graham distinguish the exclusive right from the public end it serves. A supported systemic transition or conditioned preservation program could differ. Delayed verbatim dissemination is a real speech burden, but his Copyright Clause ground supplies complete relief without a universal independent speech tier. He preserves Campbell’s actual fair-use analysis.

He would reverse, declare the added twenty years invalid within the challenged application, and remand for severability and prospective relief against federal enforcement. Relief would presumptively cover the proved unconstitutional existing-term extension unless the defendant establishes that a narrower cure fully remedies it, within severability and the court’s authority over that defendant. The decree would use preextension expiration dates: lawful earlier terms survive, and works with unexpired earlier terms do not immediately enter the public domain. He would leave the new-work term intact and award no damages or personal judgments against absent owners. Under Harper the rule would govern the parties and cases still on direct review; Reynoldsville preserves independent immunity, procedural, and remedial limits, while reliance alone would not justify selective prospectivity. Final judgments would remain closed, and collateral relief would require its own authorization. Private infringement disputes would apply the constitutional rule through their own parties, defenses, and procedures.

**Stevens, dissenting.** He would reverse as to existing copyrights because completed creation cannot be induced by prolonging an existing federal bargain at the public’s expense. General parity, dissemination, and preservation rationales do not justify a blanket further restriction on the access originally promised. Initial establishment of the federal copyright system differs from enlarging an existing federal grant, and repeated legislation cannot itself establish constitutional power. Feist’s real constitutional boundary and Campbell’s and Quality King’s operative access limits support this objection. He would remand for relief confined to the existing-term application, without independently deciding First Amendment invalidity, a permissible new-work term, public-domain restoration, or remedies for older extensions. His existing-bargain ground is distinct from Stone-Zsela’s acceptance of genuinely justified later adjustments and Breyer’s assessment of this increment’s burdens and benefits.

**Breyer, dissenting.** He would reverse the challenged existing-term application under the Copyright Clause read in light of expressive interests. Meaningful legislative leeway remains, but a substantial additional restraint on use of already-created material must have an adequate copyright-related public justification. The reasons offered do not sufficiently connect this increment of exclusion to progress. Corley’s attention to functional benefit and actual expressive burden and Quality King’s preservation of operative limits support that assessment. His ground does not require a measured new-creation incentive for every work and does not impose a universal independent First Amendment tier. He would remand for relief confined to the existing-term challenge. This ground adopts no new economic calculation, aggregate royalty estimate, orphan-work count, or empirical preservation finding.'''
e['procedure'] = 'The judgment dismissing the constitutional challenge is affirmed. The existing-term extension remains enforceable, and the requested declaratory and injunctive relief against that application is denied. The unchallenged new-work term and the distinct treatment of works already in the public domain receive no disposition.'
e['sources'] = '537 U.S. 186; No. 01-618; U.S. Const., Art. I, §8, cl. 8; First Amendment; 17 U.S.C. §§102(b), 107, 302, 304. The challenge concerns the additional twenty years for subsisting copyrights, not adjudication of any particular infringement or fair-use defense. Historical legislative extensions are legislative practice, not prior judgments deciding this constitutional challenge.'
e['stone_audit'] = 'Stone’s express primary position rejects this undifferentiated existing-term increment and requires reverse/remand with the stated relief and transition limits. The conditional acceptance of a finite extension with a reasonably supported systemwide purpose does not authorize conversion of that fixed application into affirmance merely because six colleagues assess the same harmonization/preservation reasons differently. No changed operative premise or prior controlling term-extension rule activates that branch. His genuine rational-relationship constraint is compatible with Part I’s abstract recognition of a progress limit, but he expressly rejects Part I’s application, so he neither joins Part I nor qualifies as an author of the full opinion. Part II-A is severable and exactly supported by his express anti-immunity proposition and actual-burden reasoning; he joins it alone. Part II-B’s rejection of the presented speech challenge is not attributed to him, because he resolves the case through the Copyright Clause and approves no independent speech disposition. The separate writing carries his material proposed relief, burden, limits, and transition. No standing fallback is used.'
e['compatibility'] = 'The six frozen affirming Associates join Parts I, II-A, and II-B because the opinion supplies a real system-related rational connection, acknowledges the burden, preserves genuine fair use, and distinguishes Corley rather than claiming immunity. Stone’s Part II-A join changes no Associate commitment. Stevens and Breyer retain different sufficient Copyright Clause objections and do not join Stone’s proposed test or remedy by inference; each authors his own noncontrolling writing. No Marks inquiry is necessary: six directly join every judgment-supporting proposition. The historical seven-to-two result becomes six-to-three because Stone occupies the Chief’s seat and applies his approved primary ground; no Associate departs from a reconciled commitment.'
e['departure'] = '**Historical departure:** The judgment sustaining the existing-term extension has six supporters rather than the historical seven because Stone-Zsela, replacing the historical Chief Justice, concludes that the actual unconditioned retrospective increment lacks a genuine connection to progress on the asserted justifications. This is his approved Copyright Clause application; the six affirming Associates and the distinct Stevens and Breyer grounds retain their reconciled positions.'
cases.append(e)

